Docs search result (read-only, nothing built/posted). Short version: there is good documentation on pieces (ocrmypdf performance/memory, PaddleOCR detector size settings, ORT threading, DPI guidance), but no authoritative guide that covers "OCR a 300-page book with PP-OCR on a GPU via DirectML", and no per-page numbers I'd trust for our setup.

Sourced items:
1. PaddleOCR detector size (answers the DPI question). Default det_limit_type=max, det_limit_side_len=960: the longest side of the detector input is capped at 960 (images are only shrunk, not enlarged); it must be a multiple of 32; for high-resolution input they show 1216 as the example "larger resolution" setting. https://www.paddleocr.ai/main/en/version2.x/ppocr/blog/inference_args.html (also rec_image_shape default "3,48,320", rec_batch_num default 6, use_mp default off with total_process_num 6) and https://www.paddleocr.ai/v3.5.0/en/version2.x/legacy/python_infer.html. Compare ours: canvas 1152x1536 (long side 1536) and we upscale up to 2x, with a 200-dpi render capped at 4500 px long side. So our detector input has about 2.5x the pixels of the default (1536^2 vs 960^2) and 1.6x vs the 1216 example. Inference (not sourced): this is a free speed knob; most body text on a 200-dpi page is large enough at 960-1216 long side, small text (the dense credits/syllabus pages) is the accuracy risk, so test 1216 vs 1536 on his three pages before choosing. Also render DPI: the detector never needs more than its long-side cap, so rendering at 200 dpi only costs render time and preprocessing; rendering to the detector size (+ a higher-res copy only for recognizer crops) would be cheaper (inference).
2. Recognizer crop height is fixed at 48 px, default width 320 and batch 6 (same Paddle page). Our choice of 48 height is standard; the 6-line default batch is the CPU default, not a GPU recommendation.
3. ocrmypdf documentation (Tesseract, but the only serious "many pages" guide): https://ocrmypdf.readthedocs.io/en/stable/performance.html. Takeaways: parallelism is per page across worker processes (--jobs), each job pays its own peak; memory is set by the pixels of the largest page, about 15 bytes per pixel per concurrent job (a 34 MP page ~500 MB with 1 job, ~2 GB with 4); cap OCR image size with --max-ocr-image-mpixels (their example 8) because Tesseract is tuned to ~300 dpi and gains little above 400; speed settings: skip optimisation, avoid force-ocr and preprocessing. No pages/min figure on that page. Related: https://ocrmypdf.readthedocs.io/en/stable/advanced.html (skip-big, time limits) and a user report of 500 pages in 20-30 min for invoice PDFs, Tesseract: https://github.com/ocrmypdf/OCRmyPDF/issues/220 (old, CPU).
4. ocrmypdf-paddleocr-plus: one PaddleOCR model per process and batched page inference, plus --jobs and --paddle-batch-size knobs (documents the "load once, batch pages" pattern, no numbers): https://pypi.org/project/ocrmypdf-paddleocr-plus/. This matches what we do (one worker with a loaded model per job).
5. ONNX Runtime CPU tuning: intra_op_num_threads default is tuned, ORT multithreads inside ops, so outer batching adds little on CPU; a recent PR to MLAS reports near-linear scaling of conv ops with intra-op threads at large batches/groups: https://onnxruntime.ai/docs/performance/tune-performance/threading.html and https://github.com/microsoft/onnxruntime/issues/25152. Combined with the oar-ocr measurement (bigger outer batches hurt CPU) already sent: batch for GPU, don't batch for CPU.
6. DPI guidance from other engines: Tesseract "works best at 300 dpi" https://tesseract-ocr.github.io/tessdoc/ImproveQuality.html; Internet Archive scans books at 600 dpi (https://help.archive.org/help/books-and-texts-tips-troubleshooting/), their OCR (Tesseract-based, hOCR) is described at https://archive.org/developers/ocr.html. These are about input scan quality, not PP-OCR render settings; they do not give pages/min.
7. Throughput numbers I found are vendor-blog level, low reliability, so I would not quote them to him as facts: gigagpu.com claims PaddleOCR roughly 15 pages/min CPU vs 120 pages/min on an RTX 3090 (https://gigagpu.com/paddleocr-vs-tesseract-vs-easyocr/), another blog claims PaddleOCR mobile ~86 pages/min on CPU (https://universeblend.blog/trocr-vs-tesseract-vs-paddleocr-ocr-model-2/). They contradict each other 6x and don't state page density or CPU, so they only show "tens of pages per minute" is the order of magnitude people claim.

What I did NOT find: a write-up covering checkpoint/resume, engine recycling, or warm-up amortisation for ORT/DML batch OCR; any Internet-Archive-style pipeline detail on batching; sourced guidance on aspect-ratio sorting beyond the RapidOCR docs I sent earlier (secondary deepwiki pages). Those parts of our design (per-page progress, child process recycling, resume by skipping pages with text) are our own engineering, not documented practice.

Actionable from this: (a) test detector long side 960/1216 instead of 1536 and drop the 2x upscale (PaddleOCR never upscales by default), and render at about the detector size; (b) sort lines by width (already done) and batch large on GPU only; (c) keep one long-lived worker per job (matches the ocrmypdf plugin pattern); (d) memory budget about 15 B/pixel rule from ocrmypdf is a reasonable sanity check for our ~900 MB worker observed under Wine (a 200-dpi A4 page is 3.9 MP). All speed claims about his GPU stay unverified until his PC runs it.
EOF
